</ul><h1>Handles Mean Business</h1><p>When you really overdo it with a fast food order, you’ll know.</p>
<p>It’s not just because the total has you wondering if you couldn’t have gone to a sit-down restaurant instead. And it’s definitely not because the drive-through worker gives you a judgmental look. These are professionals, and they would never. Kind of like how we never whisper behind the scenes about those of you who jump all over the semi-expired ramen noodles here every time they’re offered.</p>
<p>It’s none of that.</p>
<p>It’s the handle.</p>
<p>A handle on a fast food bag is the universal symbol that you’re either buying for a Cub Scout troop or you have really overdone it personally.</p>
<p>But that isn’t the only time handles indicate that things have been kicked up a notch. The big detergent jug that’s going to get you through more than a week of laundry? Handle. Buying an amount of paint that moves from “touch-up project” to “proper painting job”? Handle. A serious haul at the clothing store? Handle. (Rope handle if you’ve really messed up.)</p>
<p>Which brings us to this portable power station.</p>
<p>Complete with…a handle.</p>
<p>That matters because a handle means this has officially left the realm of a tiny rectangle you panic-bought at an airport kiosk–or <em>cough</em> ordered from here. This is for when the power needs have multiplied, or when your upcoming period of outletlessness stretches longer than an hour or two. Think phones, tablets, earbuds, a speaker, and whatever other strange rechargeable doodads modern life has placed into your orbit.</p>
<p>Then there’s the solar panel, which is where the whole thing starts to feel less like “charging some stuff” and more like “setting up a tiny off-grid utility station near your picnic blanket.” No, you aren’t powering a moon base. But you <em>are</em> bringing backup power that can be topped off by the sun, which is a pretty good sign that you have moved beyond asking whether anyone remembered the charging cable.</p>
<p>So, is this overkill for one phone having one little battery crisis? Probably. But for travel, camping, outages, tailgates, backyard hangs, or any situation where everyone’s devices start stumbling into low battery mode at the same time, a little overkill is kind of the point.</p>
<p>In conclusion: handle.</p>